The conv step of the gated delta net: four chains in flight (lab proposal)

The single-token conv is the input path of every gated delta net layer, and the decode profile on the
phone measures it at 245 us per layer. In the lab it costs 592.3 cycles for each block of 32 channels
and commits 0.66 instructions per packet, where a packet holds 4. COPROC_BUSY is 51.3 % of its
cycles.

The slot holds 3 floats for each channel and the arithmetic needs one lane for each channel, thus
the kernel expands 3 lanes to 4 with a masked rotate for each channel of a window and reverses
that at the end: 83 permutes for each block. The permute unit takes 1 operation per packet, thus
those permutes alone are a floor of 166 cycles. The measured 592.3 is 3.6 times that floor,
because each window is one chain of dependent operations and a result is ready only 2 packets
after its producer.

The two alternatives are measured and both fail:
  - A cheaper permute does not exist. tools/htp-lab (target perm3) applies vdelta and vrdelta with
    the control i ^ j at each output byte to all 21 lane patterns that this kernel could want, and
    every one of them is wrong in 4 to 21 of its 32 lanes: those networks realize power-of-2
    strides only, thus the masked rotate is the available primitive and not a naive choice.
  - A plane layout would remove almost every permute, but the slot is the recurrent state cache.
    The graph of src/models/delta-net-base.cpp builds it as a CONCAT of [d_conv - 1, n_ch], every
    chain that the Hexagon matcher refuses reads it with the generic ops, and
    llama_memory_recurrent::state_write_data writes those bytes into the state file that the app
    stores. Thus the host, the CPU reference and the state file would have to change together,
    and at one token a conversion at the two ends costs the same permutes again.

What is left is the dependency structure, and that is what this patch changes. The four windows of
a block are independent, thus the kernel takes one step of all four before the next step of any.
That keeps four chains in flight for three more live vectors.

Measured in the simulator, one thread, n_ch 6144, 20 iterations, against the same program without
the patch: 592.3 to 398.4 cycles for each block, 1.49 times faster, 53.85 us to 36.22 us for the
whole op (8.97 us to 6.04 us at 6 threads). The output is bit-identical: the slot bytes match
exactly and y keeps the same maximum error of 9.54e-07 against the scalar reference.

Two variants are measured and rejected. A tree over the eight terms of a window shortens the chain
further but needs eight live vectors for each window, and the kernel then spills: 1472 cycles for
each block, with HVX_LD_L2_OUTSTANDING at 19.8 %. Two blocks in one iteration give 401.5 cycles,
which is no gain, and COPROC_BUSY returns to 49 %, thus the permutes become the limit.

